;
; The import library every spicule PE links against.  ntdll.dll is the
; only DLL spicule imports from at all (KERNEL32=no is the default; see
; `make check-kernel32`), so this file is the complete statement of what
; the library asks of the operating system -- and therefore the complete
; statement of which Windows versions it can run on.
;
; That is why every name below carries the NTDLL version it was first
; exported from.  A static import of a name the running ntdll does not
; export is not a call that fails: the loader refuses the whole image
; before a single instruction of it runs.  So the library's minimum
; supported Windows version is the MAXIMUM of these annotations, and a
; single unannotated or careless addition here silently raises the floor
; for every program built against spicule.  tools/lint-minver.sh is the
; guard: it fails if any export lacks an annotation, or if the maximum
; annotation is not the floor declared in README.md.
;
; Today that maximum is 6.0 -- Vista -- imposed by NtCreateThreadEx,
; RtlCloneUserProcess, RtlCreateProcessParametersEx and RtlExitUserProcess
; (fork/exec).  It used to be 6.1 (Windows 7), from RtlUTF8ToUnicodeN and
; RtlUnicodeToUTF8N (the UTF-8 <-> UTF-16 conversion every path-taking
; function goes through); src/internal/utf.c no longer imports either --
; it converts in-tree now -- so that floor dropped out from under it.
;
; The versions are Geoff Chappell's per-release "exports added for NTDLL
; x.y" lists, which is the only systematic public record of this:
;
;   3.51 baseline  https://www.geoffchappell.com/studies/windows/win32/ntdll/history/names351.htm
;   4.0            .../history/names40.htm
;   5.0  (2000)    .../history/names50.htm
;   5.1  (XP)      .../history/names51.htm
;   5.2  (2003)    .../history/names52.htm   ("begins from SP1" for the three named here)
;   6.0  (Vista)   .../history/names60.htm
;   6.1  (7)       .../history/names61.htm
;   6.2  (8)       .../history/names62.htm
;   6.3  (8.1)     .../history/names63.htm
;   10.0 (10)      .../history/names100.htm
;
; Each name below appears on exactly one of those pages.  The pages carry
; no #-anchors, so there is nothing finer to cite than the page.

LIBRARY ntdll.dll

EXPORTS
